  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 528 AM EDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nQuiet weather today into the afternoon on Friday, with light winds \nand partly to mostly sunny skies thanks to high pressure overhead. \nHighs will be in the 80s, with lows in the 60s. Expect increasing \nclouds by Friday afternoon as an upper level trough and surface low \ndrops into IA/WI/IL. This low will drift east-southeastward through \nIN/OH/KY Friday evening into Sunday and become more of an elongated \ntrough over our area, bringing chances for showers and storms. As \nmentioned in the previous discussion, moisture transport ahead of \nthe next system may be slower to arrive than earlier guidance was \nsuggesting, and so have most of the pops after 6 pm EDT on Friday, \nincreasing from west to east roughly through Saturday morning. The \nmain concern with this system is heavy rain, but the system should \nmove fast enough to preclude any significant flooding issues. At \nthis point, best instability looks to remain south of our area, with \nmid level lapse rates across the CWA only around 5-6 C/Km and \nlimited surface instability (we have around 200-400 j/kg at times, \nbest on Saturday afternoon). 0-6km bulk effective shear does reach \naround 20-35 knots at times, but with the weaker mid level lapse \nrates and lower sfc instability don't think we'll have any kind of \nsevere threat (SPC keeps us in general thunder Sat, with a marginal \nrisk just south of our CWA (closest to Jay County, IN). This makes \nsense with the better instability axis south. Otherwise, lingering \nchances for showers/storms persist south of US 24 on Sunday (only \nhave a 20 percent chance).\n\nAs the low moves through expect winds to shift more to the east-\nnortheast through Sunday, with more northerly flow expected on Lake \nMichigan towards Sun afternoon. Gusts up to around 20-25 mph are \npossible Sun, with waves building up to around 3-5 feet (highest \nwaves at Lake MI beaches along southern Berrien County, MI and La \nPorte County, IN). Expect strong longshore/structural currents, \nparticularly on the north sides of piers/breakwalls. Rip currents \nare also possible within sandbars. Have a high swim risk forecast \nfor Sunday in both counties right now, but if winds are lighter or \nmore E-NE than forecast then waves may not climb as high. A beach \nhazards statement may eventually be needed. On Saturday, more E-NE \nflow (lighter than Sunday) could bring waves up to around 2-3 feet \nalong La Porte County beaches, so have a moderate swim risk \nforecasted. Bottom line: if heading to the beaches along Lake MI \nthis weekend, make sure you check the forecast before you go for any \nstatements on conditions. Heed the advice of lifeguards and pay \nattention to beach flags.",
